// surf -- the drawing surface.
//
// One flat int[] of sf_gw * sf_gh packed 0xRRGGBB pixels, row-major, top row
// first. That layout is not a choice: it is exactly what backends/gfx's
// id_gfx_present blits, so the engine's framebuffer and the window's are the
// same object and presenting costs one copy in C.
//
// **The surface is the window, pixel for pixel.** It is re-sized to whatever
// gfx_width/gfx_height report, every frame, and nothing is magnified on the way
// out: one framebuffer cell is one screen pixel. What a game *declares* as its
// display is no longer the size of this buffer -- it is the size of the stage
// that gets mapped into it (sys_stage), which is the same relationship the 3D
// pipeline already has to the world.
//
// Two rules of the language shape how a resize is possible at all:
//
//   * only the *declaring* function may assign an exported scalar, so sf_init --
//     which declares sf_gw and sf_gh -- has to be the function a resize calls.
//     Re-running an `export` declaration re-assigns it, which is exactly what is
//     wanted here.
//   * a list only ever grows, and `lst_fill` on an already-declared list grows it
//     **in place**. So the framebuffer is declared once, by sf_alloc, and every
//     later size only extends it. Re-declaring it per resize would abandon the
//     old one, and `id` frees nothing: dragging a window across five hundred
//     sizes would retain five hundred framebuffers.
//
// The buffer is therefore a high-water mark. A window grown to 3840x2160 keeps
// 8.3 M cells for the rest of the run even if it is shrunk again; that is the
// honest cost of a language without free, and it is bounded by the largest window
// the user actually opened.

sf_init(int w, int h) {
  export int sf_gw = w;
  export int sf_gh = h;
  sf_init2(w, h);
} return void;
